r` → P1 (HLR-4 is P2 in\nthe HLR table, but the docs-authoring priority exception governs the epic).\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 — per-project Kueue GPU quota data (DW)\"] --> E003[\"E003 — consumption vs quota admin view\"]\n    E002[\"E002 — consumption reports + CSV/API (Dashboard)\"] --> E003\n    E001 --> E004[\"E004 — report semantics & export docs\"]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E003 → E004 (and E002 → E003 → E004), length 3.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (build-against artifact) | The quota-comparison view's code builds against the per-project quota surface E001 exposes (an API/CR/metrics surface, not a configuration reference). |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 12/13 + shared-surface rule | The quota view displays current-month consumption, which is the report capability over a fixed time range; the edge prevents two epics building the same consumption-data surface independently. |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built, including the quota data semantics. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers report semantics and export integration. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation covers the consumption-vs-quota view. |\n\nNon-edges, deliberately:\n\n- **No E001 → E002 edge**: the P0 reporting work does not consume the quota\n  surface; keeping them parallel prevents a P1 enabler from blocking all P0\n  work (priority-inversion guard).\n- **No edge to/from RHAISTRAT-9990**: it is another strategy, not an epic in\n  this DAG. Its schema finality is expressed as ACs (conformance gate) and\n  Risks on E002/E003, per the Rule 21 \"third party resolves\" pattern plus\n  tiered delivery.\n- **No Investigation epic**: see Open Question Evaluation above.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 | Per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E002 (bundled) — CSV export and stable API retrieval are access modes of the same per-project report surface HLR-1 builds; a split would build the same report data surface twice and serialize presentation work behind it, so it is not separately schedulable. | Export + API stay with the report computation. |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 (quota-data enabler), E003 (admin view) | Quota side (E001) and the Dashboard comparison view (E003). |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Documentation of report semantics and export integration. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): Affected Components has no\n   per-component change descriptions or owners; Impacted Teams table,\n   Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are\n   absent. Epic scopes, teams, and ACs were derived from the HLRs and the\n   architecture-context owning teams. Human verification of the inferred\n   scopes is recommended at review.\n2. **External dependency timing risk** (the strategy's open question): the\n   metering data schema is owned by RHAISTRAT-9990 and is not final until\n   that strategy lands. No epic is invented for it (it resolves itself);\n   conformance is an AC gate on E002/E003 and the semantics risk is noted on\n   E004. Timing is outside this strategy's control — a schedule risk, not an\n   acceptance criterion.\n3. **Architecture tension on the reporting data path** (scope trap): the\n   Dashboard architecture context states the Dashboard \"owns no data plane\"\n   and its interfaces are \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\".\n   HLR-1/HLR-2 require computing reports from non-CR metering data and\n   exposing a stable API for finance tooling. The strategy does not specify\n   the consumption/access surface; the decomposition keeps this at functional\n   level (consume the metering delivery as provided by RHAISTRAT-9990) and\n   flags the ungrounded API surface rather than inventing one.\n4. **Inferred Distributed Workloads scope**: the strategy lists Distributed\n   Workloads as affected but describes no change for it. HLR-3's\n   per-project Kueue quota comparison is the only DW-adjacent requirement,\n   and current Kueue metrics (pending/admitted workloads per queue) do not\n   expose per-project GPU quota usage, so E001 scopes that exposure. If\n   review determines the Dashboard can read sufficient quota data directly\n   from existing Kueue CRs, E001 reduces to a validation acceptance\n   criterion on E003.\n5. **Inferred docs team**: no Impacted Teams table exists; E004 is assigned\n   to the RHOAI Dashboard team as the primary delivering team for the\n   documented capability.\n6. **Priority inversions**: none found. E002 (P0) blocks no P0-lower work;\n   E001/E003 (P1) do not gate any P0 epic.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nExternal dependency with uncertain timing: the metering data schema owned by\nRHAISTRAT-9990 (in flight). Can the strategy deliver partial value without\nthe final schema? **Yes** — the metering data is already being delivered by\nthe in-flight strategy, so:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent work, starts now)**: E001 (fully independent of\n  metering data — quota side only), E002 and E003 implemented and validated\n  against the metering data as currently delivered.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: final-schema conformance — once RHAISTRAT-9990\n  lands, the reporting output is re-validated against the finalized schema\n  and the documentation reflects the final report semantics. This gate is\n  expressed as acceptance criteria on E002/E003 (and a semantics AC on E004),\n  not as separate epics. Fallback if the final schema diverges: adapt the\n  reporting consumption layer (fix-and-retry); the reporting surfaces and\n  quota work are unaffected.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"st