s.\n146:24. `docs-authoring` epic → add \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC\n171:| `docs-authoring` | Red Hat docs content process — specialized tooling |\n291:   The four section headings are literal and required for EVERY epic, including `docs-authoring` epics — an outline of content topics goes INSIDE the `## Scope` section, never as a renamed `## Content Outline` heading (a renamed heading fails the artifact contract).\n349:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), and implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead); and work-type-extracted enforcement deliverables (a general Implementation epic titled as a lint rule / CI check / static-analysis check / validation harness that shares its component with another non-docs Implementation epic — fold the enforcement work back into every epic whose surface it covers, as Scope plus a verification AC, and delete the standalone epic). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88b1939eca94a553","agent_id":"a2bc11baaad4a4e6d","session_id":"bb15c9ed-6003-4c9d-b6c4-e899274a0ed5","uuid":"0cec8043-7541-4bd7-93d6-1909901e687a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T20:45:21.692Z","tool_result_meta":[{"